Not necessarily. While every dependency requires an execution environment, they do not all require an interpreter. Whether a dependency needs an interpreter depends entirely on how that dependency was built and the programming language it uses.
In modern software development, dependencies generally fall into three distinct categories based on how they handle execution:

1. Interpreted Dependencies (Require an Interpreter)

If a software project pulls in a dependency written in an interpreted script language, that dependency must pass through the host language's interpreter to run.
• Example: If a Node.js betting site uses an open-source npm package to handle real-time sports score mathematics, that package consists of raw JavaScript files. When the app boots up, the server's V8 engine interpreter reads both the main application code and the dependency code together.
• Other Examples: Python packages (pip), PHP libraries (composer), and Ruby gems.

2. Compiled Dependencies (Do Not Require an Interpreter)

If a dependency is written in a language like C, C++, or Rust, it is compiled directly into native machine code (binary code) before it is ever imported into the project.
• How it runs: These are called compiled binaries or shared libraries (like .so files on Linux or .dll files on Windows).
• The Workflow: When the main application calls this dependency, the instructions go directly to the CPU. There is no interpreter or virtual machine standing in the middle to translate the code line-by-line, because the code is already in the CPU's native tongue (1s and 0s). Platforms use these for ultra-fast tasks, like heavy cryptographic hashing or high-speed order matching.

3. Managed/Bytecode Dependencies (Require a Virtual Machine Runtime)

Some dependencies sit in a middle ground. They are pre-compiled, but not into native machine code. Instead, they are compiled into bytecode.
• How it runs: These dependencies require a Virtual Machine (VM) runtime to execute them. The VM acts as a highly optimized, specialized interpreter.
• Example: A Java archive (.jar file) used as a dependency in an enterprise backend is compiled bytecode. When the application runs, the Java Virtual Machine (JVM) interprets or Just-In-Time (JIT) compiles that bytecode into native machine instructions on the fly.

